# What `gcloud builds submit` uploads as the studio image's build context
# (infra/studio-cloudbuild.yaml). Presence of this file means .gitignore is NOT
# consulted -- list every heavy/irrelevant path explicitly.
#
# READ THAT SECOND SENTENCE AS A SECURITY PROPERTY, NOT A CONVENIENCE NOTE (ADR-0544).
# apps/studio/Dockerfile is `COPY . .`, so whatever survives this filter is baked into
# a published image, unread. Because .gitignore is bypassed here, a path being
# gitignored buys NOTHING -- every credential-shaped path must be repeated below or it
# ships. This file, not a .dockerignore, is the control: there is no .dockerignore and
# none is wanted, because the documented build path is `gcloud builds submit`.
#
# The exposure this guards is the BY-HAND build (the runbook in studio-cloudbuild.yaml's
# header), which uploads a working tree as it actually is. The CI path
# (.github/workflows/deploy-studio.yml) checks out clean, so only committed files exist
# there and this filter has nothing private to catch.

# -- Credential + private runtime material: the reason this file is security-relevant --
# Mirrors .gitignore's "Env / secrets" block line for line. .env.example tells you to
# copy it to .env and paste an API key in, and .gitignore then makes that feel safe.
# It is only safe here because of the next three lines. Keeping the two lists in step is no longer
# left to memory: `pnpm check:gcloudignore-mirror` reds the gate when they diverge (see below).
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
# App-owned runtime state under apps/studio/data/ — member identities and attestations
# (ADR-0043/0044) and the runtime asset view that replaced the retired committed assets.json.
# Gitignored, never committed, and must not be baked into a published image either.
#
# ⚠ THIS LIST IS NOW MECHANICALLY HELD TO .gitignore'S — `pnpm check:gcloudignore-mirror`
# (ADR-0544 D5). `assets.runtime.json` is here because that rung found it missing the day it was
# built: it had been added to .gitignore alone, which is the exact silent drift the rung exists to
# stop. Add a credential- or runtime-state-shaped path to .gitignore and the gate will ask for it
# here on the same branch.
apps/studio/data/users.json
apps/studio/data/attestations.json
apps/studio/data/assets.runtime.json
# Terraform operator material, kept out of git by a NESTED ignore file — infra/.gitignore, whose own
# comment says terraform.tfvars "Holds operator_email — local only", and which also ignores local
# state (single operator, no remote backend) and run logs. Found 2026-09-24: none of these was
# excluded here, and the main checkout holds an infra/terraform.tfvars, so a by-hand build from it
# would have baked the operator's email into the image.
#
# ⚠ THESE LINES ARE HELD BY HAND, NOT BY `pnpm check:gcloudignore-mirror`: that rung reads only the
# top-level .gitignore, so it never saw infra/.gitignore. Whether this file should keep copying
# ignore lines at all — or read .gitignore directly, or a by-hand build should upload committed
# files only — is the owner's open question `oq-image-filter-stops-copying-gitignore`
# (`two-copies-held-by-a-checker-arc`). Until it is answered, a new line in infra/.gitignore needs
# its twin here.
infra/terraform.tfvars
infra/*.tfstate
infra/*.tfstate.*
infra/.terraform-tmp/
infra/apply.log

# -- Heavy / irrelevant --
.git
.gitmodules
legacy/
node_modules/
**/node_modules/
.claude/
apps/studio/dist/
apps/studio/.studio.log
apps/studio/.studio.pid
apps/studio/test-results/
apps/studio/playwright-report/
infra/functions/
infra/.terraform/
